H1972.strongs:
Origin: probably denominatively from 1973;
Meaning: to remove or be remote
KJV renders: cast far off.

H1973.strongs:
Origin: from the primitive form of the article (hal);
Meaning: to the distance, i.e. far away; also (of time) thus far
KJV renders: back, beyond, (hence,-) forward, hitherto, thence, forth, yonder.

H1974.strongs:
Origin: from 1984 (in the sense of rejoicing);
Meaning: a celebration of thanksgiving for harvest
KJV renders: merry, praise.

H1975.strongs:
Origin: from 1976;
Meaning: this or that
KJV renders: side, that, this.

H1976.strongs:
Origin: from the article (see 1973) and 2088;
Meaning: this very
KJV renders: this.

H1977.strongs:
Origin: another form of 1976;
Meaning: that
KJV renders: this.

H1978.strongs:
Origin: from 1980;
Meaning: a walk, i.e. (by implication) a step
KJV renders: step.

H1979.strongs:
Origin: feminine of 1978;
Meaning: a walking; by implication, a procession or march, a caravan
KJV renders: company, going, walk, way.

H1980.strongs:
Origin: akin to 3212; a primitive root;
Meaning: to walk (in a great variety of applications, literally and figuratively)
KJV renders: (all) along, apace, behave (self), come, (on) continually, be conversant, depart, be eased, enter, exercise (self), follow, forth, forward, get, go (about, abroad, along, away, forward, on, out, up and down), greater, grow, be wont to haunt, lead, march, × more and more, move (self), needs, on, pass (away), be at the point, quite, run (along), send, speedily, spread, still, surely, tale-bearer, travel(-ler), walk (abroad, on, to and fro, up and down, to places), wander, wax, (way-) faring man, × be weak, whirl.

H1981.strongs:
Origin: (Aramaic) corresponding to 1980 (compare 1946);
Meaning: to walk
KJV renders: walk.

H1982.strongs:
Origin: from 1980;
Meaning: properly, a journey, i.e. (by implication) a wayfarer; also a flowing
KJV renders: × dropped, traveller.

H1983.strongs:
Origin: (Aramaic) from 1981;
Meaning: properly, a journey, i.e. (by implication) toll on goods at a road
KJV renders: custom.

H1984.strongs:
Origin: a primitive root;
Meaning: to be clear (orig. of sound, but usually of color); to shine; hence, to make a show, to boast; and thus to be (clamorously) foolish; to rave; causatively, to celebrate; also to stultify
KJV renders: (make) boast (self), celebrate, commend, (deal, make), fool(-ish, -ly), glory, give (light), be (make, feign self) mad (against), give in marriage, (sing, be worthy of) praise, rage, renowned, shine.

H1985.strongs:
Origin: from 1984; praising (namely God);
Meaning: Hillel, an Israelite
KJV renders: Hillel.

H1986.strongs:
Origin: a primitive root;
Meaning: to strike down; by implication, to hammer, stamp, conquer, disband
KJV renders: beat (down), break (down), overcome, smite (with the hammer).

H1987.strongs:
Origin: from 1986; smiter;
Meaning: Helem, the name of two Israelites
KJV renders: Helem.

H1988.strongs:
Origin: from the article (see 1973);
Meaning: hither
KJV renders: here, hither(-(to)), thither.

H1989.strongs:
Origin: from 1986;
Meaning: a hammer (or mallet)
KJV renders: hammer.

H1990.strongs:
Origin: of uncertain derivation;
Meaning: Ham, a region of Palestine
KJV renders: Ham.

H1991.strongs:
Origin: from 1993;
Meaning: abundance, i.e. wealth
KJV renders: any of theirs.

H1992.strongs:
Origin: or (prolonged) הֵמָּה; masculine plural from 1931;
Meaning: they (only used when emphatic)
KJV renders: it, like, × (how, so) many (soever, more as) they (be), (the) same, × so, × such, their, them, these, they, those, which, who, whom, withal, ye.

H1993.strongs:
Origin: a primitive root (compare 1949);
Meaning: to make a loud sound (like English 'hum'); by implication, to be in great commotion or tumult, to rage, war, moan, clamor
KJV renders: clamorous, concourse, cry aloud, be disquieted, loud, mourn, be moved, make a noise, rage, roar, sound, be troubled, make in tumult, tumultuous, be in an uproar.

H1994.strongs:
Origin: (Aramaic) or (prolonged) הִמּוֹן; (Aramaic), corresponding to 1992;
Meaning: they
KJV renders: × are, them, those.

H1995.strongs:
Origin: or הָמֹן; (Ezekiel 5:7), from 1993;
Meaning: a noise, tumult, crowd; also disquietude, wealth
KJV renders: abundance, company, many, multitude, multiply, noise, riches, rumbling, sounding, store, tumult.

H1996.strongs:
Origin: from 1995 and 1463;
Meaning: the multitude of Gog; the fanciful name of an emblematic place in Palestine
KJV renders: Hamon-gog.

H1998.strongs:
Origin: from 1993;
Meaning: sound
KJV renders: noise.

H1999.strongs:
Origin: or (too fully) הֲמוּלָּה; (Jeremiah 11:16), feminine passive participle of an unused root meaning to rush (as rain with a windy roar);
Meaning: a sound
KJV renders: speech, tumult.
